<section>
  <h2>Contact Estimation as a Distribution</h2>
  <p>
    ContactFlowFilter estimates how many contacts are active, where they occur, and the forces they exert using joint torque sensors and a
    force/torque sensor at the robot base. These measurements do not always identify a unique contact configuration. A useful estimator must retain
    multiple plausible explanations while following sliding contacts and detecting when contacts appear or disappear.
  </p>
  <figure class="research-figure--wide">
    <a href="{{ '/assets/img/research/contactflowfilter/fig2_challenges.webp' | relative_url }}">
      <img
        src="{{ '/assets/img/research/contactflowfilter/fig2_challenges.webp' | relative_url }}"
        alt="Ambiguous contact configurations, temporal changes, and contact addition or removal"
        loading="lazy"
      >
    </a>
    <figcaption>The three challenges: multiple possible solutions, temporal dependence, and discrete contact events.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>Combining Flow Matching and Particle Filtering</h2>
  <p>
    The estimator combines the temporal prediction of a particle filter with samples generated by a flow matching model. The learned proposal can
    place particles in plausible regions that ordinary resampling has missed. The particle filter then weighs those proposals against the previous
    estimate, retaining a recursive representation of the contact state.
  </p>
  <figure class="research-figure--wide">
    <a href="{{ '/assets/img/research/contactflowfilter/fig3_overview.webp' | relative_url }}">
      <img
        src="{{ '/assets/img/research/contactflowfilter/fig3_overview.webp' | relative_url }}"
        alt="Flow matching and temporal particle-filter proposals merged into a recursive contact estimate"
        loading="lazy"
      >
    </a>
    <figcaption>Dual sampling combines the particle filter's temporal context with the generative model's multimodal proposals.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <h3>What Makes the Estimator Work</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Sensor representation.</strong> A transformer processes link poses and virtual wrenches. Paired contact-point samples encode both
      single- and dual-contact states.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Few-step generation.</strong> Flow matching produces useful proposals with two integration steps and runs asynchronously from the main
      estimator.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Surface-aware prediction.</strong> Precomputed geodesic distances support particle motion along the robot surface.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Contact-event detection.</strong> Generative proposals and quadratic-program residuals jointly decide when to add or remove a particle
      set.
    </li>
  </ul>
